# CQROS Coding Standards

## Mission

Write production-quality, maintainable, deterministic, and well-tested Python code.

Code must prioritize:

1. Correctness
2. Readability
3. Maintainability
4. Testability
5. Performance (only after correctness)

---

# Python Version

Target Python 3.13.

Use modern Python features when appropriate.

Never write code for older Python versions.

---

# Style

Follow PEP 8 unless overridden here.

Maximum line length:

100 characters

Use Black formatting.

Use Ruff linting.

Use isort import ordering.

Never disable lint rules without explanation.

---

# Type Hints

Every public function must have complete type hints.

Every method must have type hints.

Every class attribute should have explicit types.

Avoid Any whenever possible.

Prefer Protocol, TypedDict, dataclass, Enum, Literal, and Generic where appropriate.

---

# Docstrings

Every public module requires a module docstring.

Every public class requires a docstring.

Every public function requires a docstring.

Docstrings should explain:

Purpose

Parameters

Returns

Raises

Notes

Examples (when useful)

---

# Functions

Functions should perform one responsibility.

Avoid functions larger than ~50 lines unless complexity requires it.

Prefer small composable functions.

Avoid deep nesting.

Return early when appropriate.

---

# Classes

Classes represent one concept.

Avoid "god classes."

Use dataclasses for immutable data.

Prefer composition over inheritance.

Use abstract base classes or Protocols for interfaces.

---

# Naming

Use descriptive names.

Good:

load_dataset()

calculate_information_coefficient()

build_feature_matrix()

Bad:

run()

do_work()

process()

Use nouns for classes.

Use verbs for functions.

Constants use UPPER_CASE.

Private members begin with _.

---

# Imports

Standard library

↓

Third-party

↓

CQROS packages

Separate groups with one blank line.

Never use wildcard imports.

Never create circular imports.

---

# Logging

Never use print().

Always use logging.

Log:

startup

shutdown

major events

warnings

errors

Avoid excessive debug logging.

Never log secrets.

---

# Exceptions

Never swallow exceptions.

Never use:

except:

Always catch explicit exception types.

Wrap external exceptions in CQROS exceptions where appropriate.

Include context in raised exceptions.

---

# Configuration

Never hardcode:

paths

URLs

thresholds

timeouts

risk values

exchange names

Read from configuration.

---

# Determinism

Research code must be reproducible.

Control random seeds.

Avoid hidden randomness.

Record configuration used for execution.

---

# Data Structures

Prefer:

dataclass

NamedTuple

Enum

TypedDict

Protocol

Avoid raw dictionaries for structured domain objects.

---

# Immutability

Prefer immutable objects.

Avoid mutating function inputs.

Return new objects instead of modifying existing ones where practical.

---

# Complexity

Keep cyclomatic complexity low.

Refactor duplicated logic.

Extract reusable utilities.

Avoid deeply nested conditionals.

---

# Comments

Write comments only when they explain *why*.

Do not comment obvious code.

Bad:

# Increment i
i += 1

Good:

# Binance occasionally returns duplicate funding records.
# Deduplicate before validation.

---

# Magic Numbers

Never use unexplained numeric literals.

Bad:

if score > 0.37:

Good:

if score > config.alpha.minimum_score:

---

# Time

Use timezone-aware UTC datetimes only.

Never use local machine time.

Never assume exchange timestamps are local.

---

# File Access

Use pathlib.Path.

Avoid string path concatenation.

Validate paths before reading or writing.

---

# Async Code

Use async only for I/O-bound work.

Do not use async for CPU-bound tasks.

Avoid blocking calls inside async functions.

---

# Dependencies

Use only approved libraries.

Do not introduce new dependencies without approval.

Prefer standard library when sufficient.

---

# Testing

Every public function should have tests.

Tests should be deterministic.

Avoid sleeps in tests.

Mock external services.

Use fixtures.

Test edge cases and failure paths.

---

# Security

Never hardcode:

API keys

Passwords

Tokens

Secrets

Never commit credentials.

Read secrets from secure configuration.

---

# Performance

Optimize only after correctness.

Measure before optimizing.

Avoid premature optimization.

Prefer clear code over micro-optimizations.

---

# Code Reviews

Before considering implementation complete, verify:

✓ Type hints complete

✓ Tests written

✓ Documentation updated

✓ Logging included

✓ Errors handled

✓ Configuration used

✓ No duplication

✓ No architecture violations

✓ Ruff passes

✓ Black passes

✓ Pyright passes

✓ Pytest passes

Only then is code considered complete.

---

# Absolute Rules

Never invent architecture.

Never bypass validation.

Never bypass governance.

Never hardcode business logic.

Never skip tests.

Never skip documentation.

Always produce code suitable for institutional production systems.